{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310566,"nonce":"sg9TvNqWzfOZmpYMi1MmR2gUU5M=","title":"Examples CI flakes ~7.5% on x86_64 Linux with TryFromIntError(NegOverflow) in cranelift-jit. Upstream gap: x86 PC-rel32 relocs have no ...","message":"**`Examples` CI fails intermittently on Linux x86_64:**\n\n```\nthread 'main' panicked at cranelift/jit/src/compiled_blob.rs:142:80:\ncalled `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: TryFromIntError(NegOverflow)\n```\n\nHits `test_core_types_e2e` at L5 (Cranelift JIT codegen), test varies (seen at\n`array_basic`).\n\n\n`compiled_blob.rs` line 142, in the `Reloc::X86PCRel4 | Reloc::X86CallPCRel4` arm:\n\n```rust\nlet pcrel = i32::try_from((what as isize) - (at as isize)).unwrap();\n```\n\n`at` is the JIT-allocated code, `what` is the runtime symbol (in the executable).\nNegOverflow ⇒ the target sits more than 2GB BELOW the call site, so the 32-bit\nPC-relative displacement does not fit.\n\n**arm64 handles exactly this case; x86 does not.** Veneers are reserved only at\nline 44 (`Reloc::Arm64Call =\u003e veneer_count += 1`) and emitted only in the\n`Arm64Call` arm (line 156), which falls back to `ldr x16 / br x16` when the\noffset exceeds ±128MB. The x86 arm has NO fallback — it unwraps and panics.\n**That is why macOS arm64 can never hit it and Linux x86_64 can.**\n\nChecked ALL FIVE wasmtime revs in `~/.cargo/git/checkouts/wasmtime-*/`\n(0aafd21, 5937fbc, 6015414 = our pin, 8cb28bc, bc2f967): every one reserves\nveneers for `Arm64Call` only. **A pin bump does NOT fix this.**\n\n`is_pic = true` is not an escape either: `Reloc::X86GOTPCRel4` and\n`X86CallPLTRel4` both `panic!` unconditionally in this JIT.\n\n\nIdentical signature (`compiled_blob.rs:142:80`) on **2026-08-29** (run\n33224623851) and **2026-09-02** (run 33673802854), i.e. long before the\n2026-09-03 parser commit it was first noticed on. **3 failures in 40 runs ≈ 7.5%.**\nRe-running the SAME failed commit (33753297624) with no code change PASSED —\nproof of non-determinism. It depends on where mmap places JIT code relative to\nthe process image, so ASLR alone flips it.\n\n**NOT REPRODUCIBLE on any local machine**: macOS arm64 25/25 twice (including\nCI's exact `--features all-backends`); NUC x86_64 Linux 4/4 with all-backends\nand 4/4 without. Any fix therefore cannot be verified locally — only by landing\nit and watching many CI runs.\n\n\n`cranelift/jit/src/backend.rs:152` takes a `Box\u003cdyn JITMemoryProvider + Send\u003e`;\nthe trait (`memory/mod.rs:29`) is just `allocate` / `free_memory` / `finalize`.\nA provider that keeps JIT code within ±2GB of the runtime symbols removes the\nfailure by construction. Caveats: `set_readable_and_executable` is `pub(crate)`,\nso the protection + icache-coherence dance must be reimplemented; and a\nwrap-and-retry-until-in-range design degrades to today's behaviour in the worst\ncase, which makes it the safer shape.\n\nOur config is at `compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs` ~196-203\n(`use_colocated_libcalls=false`, `is_pic=false`).\n\n\n`--features all-backends` (and even default features) needs LLVM:\n```\nexport LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX=/usr/lib/llvm-21\nexport RUSTFLAGS=\"-L /home/raikage/llvmstub\"    # stub libPolly.a / libPollyISL.a\n```\nThe repo's `.cargo/config.toml` pins the macOS path with `force = false`, so the\nenv var wins. Without the stub `-L`, the build dies on `could not find native\nstatic library Polly`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cranelift_jit_x86_pcrel_no_veneer` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}